# Retailer & Data Integrity

These constraints are absolute and apply regardless of what a task otherwise asks for:

- Never write, suggest, or scaffold code intended to bypass CAPTCHA, authentication, anti-bot
  systems, access controls, rate limits, `robots.txt` restrictions, or any retailer's terms of
  service. If a task implicitly or explicitly requires this, decline that part and explain why.
- Only use legitimate data sources for retailer integrations: official APIs, affiliate/partner
  APIs or feeds, product/structured feeds, or other integrations a retailer has explicitly
  permitted. Playwright/browser automation is only acceptable for a specific, named, permitted
  integration that genuinely requires it — never as a generic scraping tool.
- Never fabricate, invent, guess, or "fill in" real retailer names, prices, availability, URLs,
  or other product data. If real data isn't available, use clearly-labeled placeholder/fixture
  data in tests, and say so explicitly.
- Every retailer integration must be isolated behind the common retailer adapter interface (see
  `RETAILER_ARCHITECTURE.md`). Core logic (normalization, matching, pricing, sale-event
  intelligence, recommendation) must never branch on a specific retailer's identity.
- Every Price Observation must carry: retailer, seller, source URL, observation timestamp,
  displayed price, MRP (where available), effective price (where calculable), availability,
  source type, and a data freshness/confidence indicator. Never omit provenance fields to save
  time.
- Never merge or blur observed, calculated, and predicted values. Predicted values must always
  be clearly labeled as predictions.
